WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.681
Defenders use the Cyber Kill Chain for more than incident labels.

2
00:00:04.831 --> 00:00:12.065
It can organize timelines, map detection coverage, compare preventive controls, structure tabletop exercises, support threat intelligence reporting,

3
00:00:12.215 --> 00:00:16.045
and explain an incident to leaders in plain language.

4
00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:18.395
Its usefulness depends on flexibility.

5
00:00:18.545 --> 00:00:24.502
Modern activity may be nonlinear, cloud-native, identity-driven, automated, insider-enabled, or hidden inside legitimate tools.

6
00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:26.852
Some stages may be compressed.

7
00:00:27.002 --> 00:00:29.202
Some may repeat.

8
00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:33.182
Some may not be visible with the telemetry available.

9
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:40.140
Common mistakes include treating the model as a rigid checklist, assuming every incident must map cleanly,

10
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:46.248
equating one alert with full understanding, or focusing only on malware while ignoring identity,

11
00:00:46.398 --> 00:00:48.598
cloud, SaaS, and business-process evidence.

12
00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:52.152
The model works best when it improves questions.

13
00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:54.502
What have we observed?

14
00:00:54.652 --> 00:00:56.852
What are we inferring?

15
00:00:57.002 --> 00:00:59.202
What confidence do we have?

16
00:00:59.352 --> 00:01:01.552
Which controls helped?

17
00:01:01.702 --> 00:01:03.902
Which gaps mattered?

18
00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:06.252
What should change?

19
00:01:06.402 --> 00:01:13.636
Use it alongside incident response practice, threat modeling, telemetry engineering, control validation, intelligence analysis, and business context.

20
00:01:13.786 --> 00:01:21.446
A good Kill Chain discussion should leave the team with clearer evidence, better priorities, and defensible next steps.

21
00:01:21.596 --> 00:01:25.426
A healthy use of the model also invites disagreement.

22
00:01:25.576 --> 00:01:31.533
If one analyst maps behavior to exploitation and another sees it as delivery evidence,

23
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:36.364
the team should compare facts rather than force consensus too early.

24
00:01:36.514 --> 00:01:41.195
That discussion often reveals missing telemetry or assumptions that deserve documentation.
